nodecode-recovery-peer is a rewrite of rezaaibox-ui's rezaaibox-ui/nexus-recovery-peer:

  https://github.com/rezaaibox-ui/nexus-recovery-peer
  commit 2d3b15dcc513bab7fc2463d29fb581524a94c8fb
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Nexus (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole plugin, __init__.py, as one Common Lisp cell.
The plugin's two Hermes hooks -- pre_llm_call, which kept the turn's task, and
transform_tool_result, which counted identical results -- are one :TOOL hook
here, because that point sees the call before and the result after. The
counting is the original's: identical failing results, or identical results
from a tool whose repeats are no progress, REPEAT_THRESHOLD times in a row;
the original's 3 is kept. The review is the original's too: the same system
instruction (at most two different next steps, how to verify each, never
claim the task solved, never print credentials, no irreversible action
without approval), the same 220-token ceiling and 0 temperature, the same
best-effort redaction of PEM blocks, bearer tokens, token-prefix words and the
word after a credential label, and a suggestion appended to the result rather
than substituted for it. What the plugin asked through the host's ctx.llm
facade with a provider and model override, a cell asks through the organism's
own side call (NLE:COMPLETE) with the auxiliary target pinned to the provider
and model the user names in `peer'; the original's independence rule
(`peer_provider` must differ from `origin_provider`) survives as
`other_provider_only`, checked against the provider the session runs on. The
once-per-turn budget survives as once per stall plus a cooldown, and the
reservation before the call, which the plugin made so a batch of identical
calls asks once, is kept.

What was left out, and why: the `routes` list of {model_contains,
origin_provider, peer_provider, peer_model}, because a Nodecode cell is
told one peer in its own config section rather than matching the current
model id against a table; the delegated-child trigger, because Nodecode has no delegate_task and a
failed child arrives as a failed tool result, which
the failure branch already catches; the 18-second timeout argument, because
the organism's side call takes a token ceiling and not a deadline; and the
per-turn clock, because a cell sees no turn id at the tool point. The
plugin's own capability gate (llm.provider_override and llm.model_override,
denied means no request) has no counterpart: the cell asks only after its
section names a peer, and the peer's provider is the one it pins.
